# {{ project_name }}

Kotlin + Jetpack Compose starter for rapidly prototyping and releasing Android apps on phones and tablets{% if include_wear %}, with a separately installable Wear OS companion{% endif %}.

## First run

On Ubuntu, `bash scripts/bootstrap.sh` installs development prerequisites. Otherwise install JDK 17+, the Android SDK and [Go Task](https://taskfile.dev/), and set `ANDROID_HOME`. Then:

```sh
task setup     # install SDK packages / check local tools
task doctor    # readable environment diagnostics
task dev       # boot an emulator, install, and launch the phone app
task check     # formatting and static checks
task test      # JVM tests
task build     # debug APKs
```

`task test:ui` runs instrumented tests. `task device:inspect` and `task device:tap -- TAG` expose semantic UI nodes to coding agents. `task screenshots:preview` captures just the light-theme phone scenarios into `reports/screenshot-preview/` for quick iteration; `task screenshots` refreshes the complete light/dark phone/tablet set. Both run on demand, not in routine CI. Consult [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for implementation conventions and device tools.

`task emulator:tablet:create` creates a tablet AVD; `task emulator:tablet:start` boots it on port 5558 (select it with `ANDROID_SERIAL=emulator-5558`). {% if include_wear %}For a Wear emulator, inspect available image IDs with `sdkmanager --list`, set `ANDROID_WEAR_IMAGE` to a valid Wear OS system-image package, and run `task emulator:wear:create` and `task emulator:wear:start` (port 5556). Set `ANDROID_SERIAL=emulator-5556` before `task run:wear`. The Wear AVD uses the `wearos_small_round` hardware profile; if your SDK names it differently, run `avdmanager list device` and override `ANDROID_AVD_DEVICE` directly with `scripts/emulator.sh create`.{% endif %}

Capture device-specific review images whenever you need them: `ANDROID_SERIAL=emulator-5558 task device:screenshot -- reports/tablet.png`{% if include_wear %} or `ANDROID_SERIAL=emulator-5556 task device:screenshot -- reports/watch.png`{% endif %}. The GitHub screenshot workflow remains manual-only. Screenshots use preview-sized resolutions (540×1170 phone; 800×1200 medium tablet; 1024×1280 expanded tablet; 960×540 landscape), with density overrides chosen to preserve actual Compose layout breakpoints.

